# .sast-allowlist — triaged GitLab SAST findings that must not block the pipeline.
#
# Read by the `sast-gate` job in .gitlab-ci.yml, which drops matching findings before deciding
# pass or fail. Format: one `<semgrep-rule-id> <repo-relative-path>` pair per line, whitespace
# separated; `#` starts a comment. A malformed line is an error, not a skipped line — a typo that
# silently allowlisted nothing would be indistinguishable from a clean scan.
#
# Scope is deliberately narrow. A pair suppresses ONE rule in ONE file: the same rule firing in a
# file not listed here still fails the pipeline, and every other rule is untouched. Line numbers
# are deliberately not part of the key — they churn on every edit, and an allowlist that breaks
# whenever code moves gets widened rather than read.
#
# This is the same pattern as .gitleaks.toml and .semgrepignore, for the same reason: findings
# triaged once should stay triaged, with the reasoning recorded next to the entry.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# nodejs_scan.javascript-ssrf-rule-node_ssrf — 11 findings, triaged 2026-08-25 against pipeline
# 3296055. All false positives.
#
# The rule (njsscan, CWE-918) fires on any fetch() whose URL argument is not a string literal. It
# does not check where the value came from. SSRF requires the SERVER to be tricked into calling a
# destination an attacker chose; in every site below the host is not attacker-reachable:
#
#   * api-client.ts, agentcore-runtime-client.ts — host is a build-time constant from config
#     (getApiUrl / AGENTCORE_URL); only a path or an encodeURIComponent'd query value varies.
#   * useMetadataTracking.ts, ToolExecutionContainer.tsx — browser-side fetches to RELATIVE paths
#     ('/api/conversation/history?...', '/output/sessions/...'). There is no host to control, and
#     no server-side request is made at all.
#   * recon-auth.ts — reconFetch() is a thin auth-header wrapper; its `input` comes from the call
#     sites above, never from request data.
#
# Re-triage trigger: if any of these files starts taking a URL host from request input, delete its
# line here rather than editing this comment.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The 40 remaining Medium findings were also read, on 2026-08-25. None needs an entry here — the
# gate blocks on Critical/High only, so they are reported and not suppressed, which is the right
# resting state for findings that are false positives but worth re-reading if the code changes:
#
#   * 33 eslint.detect-non-literal-fs-filename in local-session-store.ts / local-tool-store.ts.
#     Both are development-only stores under `.local-store` in process.cwd(), reached only via a
#     dynamic import behind `if (IS_LOCAL)`. local-tool-store.ts builds no dynamic path at all
#     (every fs call uses a module-level constant); local-session-store.ts validates session, user
#     and agent ids against character allowlists and calls isPathWithinBase() before each fs call.
#   * 2 bandit.B310-1 (urllib urlopen) in backend/recon_core/gateway_client.py and
#     backend/tier1/ingress_invoke.py. Both assert the URL starts with https:// on the line above
#     and both already carry `# nosec B310` — GitLab runs a semgrep translation of the bandit rule,
#     which does not honour bandit's own suppression comment.
#   * 3 nodejs_scan.javascript-dos-rule-regex_dos in ui/JsonDisplay.tsx and lib/api-auth.ts. The
#     JsonDisplay ones render in the browser, so the blast radius is the user's own tab; the
#     api-auth one is `/^Bearer\s+(.+)$/i`, anchored with no nested quantifier.
#   * 2 nodejs_scan.javascript-crypto-rule-node_insecure_random_generator — retry-backoff jitter
#     and a React key. Neither value is a secret.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

